From f46b20313ac36ac672d6345fc6f19900a9bdd36e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 05:18:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] arrange: class support in the V_d searches
 (opts.class_support, on with msearch --diag-first)

Every vector of V_d holds exactly one of d's numbers (its class; the top
labels), so the rows (cols) of a square have the n classes, the row of
class j meeting the col of class j at d_j and every other col outside d.
At the (1,1) children, right after the support filter, CLASS_SUP drops
the candidates with a cell covered by no placed vector of the other axis
and no other-axis candidate of the right class, or that miss (outside the
placed vectors) every other-axis candidate of some unplaced class; both
axes to the fixpoint, with the support filter's death checks. The rule of
the prototype c2/vdclass 65c6495 (CLASS_SUP=6 CLASS_LVL=2 CLASS_VEC=1),
the same nodes, with its overhead removed: the lists are class-sorted, so
each class is a block and the tests are per-class unions (a zmm each);
lazy keep masks, one compaction if the node lives, incremental passes on
the cells and classes that changed, loops specialized by the number of
classes, the unions as a by-product of the passes. 9.05k cycles per call
at 13 7 4 3 1 1 / 2200 (14.7% of the search) against 12.1k (18.0%).

Active only with top_numbers of n numbers, the carried path, support and
forward checking, and every vector with exactly one top label (checked at
setup); otherwise a no-op, and the plain search and the matrix path are
unchanged (bench quick / full / prod nodes and hashes unchanged).
msearch --diag-first turns it on (--no-class-support: off, for A/B runs);
the d-first CPU per sum is 0.60-0.76x at N >= 11.7k (0.81-0.89x at
5.9-11.3k, 0.99x at 4.1k), the same (square, SP traversal) pairs.

Also: fuzz_arrange --dfirst runs every seed with it and a rotating cls_*
variant, and --noracle/--diff use the plain search's squares as the
reference; class_support_test (ctest fast_class_support_plain_root, an
ARRANGE_DEBUG build) covers the plain root, whose narrower r1 runs CLS_AT
now handles; ARRANGE_DEBUG poisons the words a narrower r1 run must not
read; CMake option MAGIC_DEFS for instrumentation builds (CLASS_PROF).

